            <h1 style="font-weight:bold;font-size:2em">
                What Islam say about giving bread to hunger
            </h1>
            <p>In Islam, the act of giving bread to the hungry is considered a virtuous and noble deed that is highly rewarded by Allah. </p>
            <p>The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) emphasized the importance of feeding the hungry and providing sustenance to those in need. It is narrated in many Hadiths that the Prophet encouraged his followers to share their food with others, stating that feeding the hungry is a means of earning the pleasure of Allah and seeking His mercy.</p>
            <p>According to Islamic teachings, feeding the hungry is not just a charitable act but a moral obligation for every believer. Muslims are urged to show compassion and generosity towards those who are less fortunate and to ensure that no one in their community goes hungry.</p>
            <p>The Quran emphasizes the significance of feeding the needy and highlights the great reward that awaits those who provide food to the hungry, stating, "And they give food in spite of love for it to the needy, the orphan, and the captive." (Quran 76:8)</p>
            <p>In Islam, giving bread to the hungry is not only a physical act of nourishment but also a spiritual practice that fosters empathy, solidarity, and gratitude. By feeding those who are hungry, Muslims demonstrate their commitment to social justice, compassion, and kindness, reflecting the values of Islam. The act of giving bread to the hungry is a profound way to fulfill the teachings of Islam, promote community cohesion, and uphold the dignity and well-being of all individuals, regardless of their circumstances.</p>

          
            <h1 style="font-weight:bold;font-size:1em">Join Us in Making a Difference.</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-3"></div>
    </div>
    @* END Section providing bread*@



